<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="injury">What Happened</h2>
<p>Daniels went down on the final play of the first half against Dallas, bracing himself with his left arm as he fell to the turf after a scramble. He was carted to the locker room with his arm in an air cast. Head coach Dan Quinn confirmed after the 37-20 loss that it was a dislocation, with X-rays showing no fracture — the same initial read the team got after Daniels' first elbow dislocation, against Seattle in Week 9 of last season.</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">🩹</div>
<div class="stat-title">Jayden Daniels — Injury Snapshot</div>
<div class="stat-sub">Week 2 vs. Dallas Cowboys · Left Elbow, Second Time</div>
<div class="stat-numbers">
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">2</div><div class="label">Same-Joint Dislocations</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">11/17</div><div class="label">Comp/Att Before Exit</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">69</div><div class="label">Rush Yards, 7 Carries</div></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Before the injury, Daniels was 11-of-17 for 96 yards and a touchdown through the air, and had already piled up 69 rushing yards on just seven carries — a snapshot of exactly the dual-threat production that made him the 2024 Offensive Rookie of the Year. Mariota took over for the second half and will start Week 3 against Seattle, going 11-of-16 for 111 yards and a touchdown in relief.</p>
<h2 id="pattern">Why This Keeps Happening</h2>
<p>Both of Daniels' elbow dislocations came the same way: bracing his fall with an outstretched arm while going to the ground at speed, either from a scramble or a hit. That's an unavoidable consequence of how often his game puts him on the ground in the open field rather than protected in the pocket. Daniels ran for over 800 yards as a rookie and remained one of the league's most dangerous scrambling threats last season even while managing the first injury — the very quality that makes him so hard to defend also means he's absorbing hits and falls that a traditional pocket passer simply never takes.</p>
<p>Last season's version of this injury cost Daniels three games initially, and he aggravated it again on his return, ultimately finishing with just seven games played. Washington went 2-6 in games Mariota started in his place. That history is exactly why the tone from the building this time is notably careful — Quinn has said the team will defer entirely to specialists on next steps rather than push for a fast return.</p>
