<h2 id="outlook">What's Next for Washington</h2>
<p>Quinn has said he expects Daniels back this season, and the lack of a fracture is a genuinely positive early sign. But given last year's pattern — an initial recovery followed by a re-aggravation that cost even more time — Washington has strong incentive to be conservative this time rather than rush Daniels back the moment he's technically clearable. The Commanders sit at 0-2 and will lean on Mariota, who has now started twice in relief of Daniels across two seasons, for at least this week against Seattle.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>The Same Trade-Off, Different Joint</strong>
  Daniels' explosiveness is real — his rookie season rushing numbers rank among the best ever for a quarterback. But that same live-arm, live-legs style of play means more falls, more hits, and more exposure for a non-throwing elbow that's now been hurt twice. It isn't a reason to change how he plays; it's the cost of the game that makes him a star.
</div>
